ous mind. This produces the feeling of being overwhelmed, overloaded, and unable to cope. This is the “overstimulation” you’re describing. So when I say, “The ADHD brain isn’t overstimulated at its core,” I don’t mean that your feeling of being overwhelmed is fake or wrong. I mean that the *mechanism creating that feeling* is a lack of neurological firepower, not an excess of it. The term “understimulation” is tied to that mechanism, not to the resulting emotion. ### A Final Analogy: The Internet Router - Imagine your home internet router has a weak power supply (low energy = **understimulation** of the router). - Because it’s underpowered, it can’t properly manage all the data packets coming from your devices. It jams up, slows down, and every webpage gives you a spinning wheel of death. - You slam your laptop shut and say, “This thing is completely **overloaded!**” And you’re right—the experience is a painful, frozen overload. - But the solution is not to send *less* data (turning off devices, which would be sedating yourself). The solution is to **plug the router into a stronger power supply** (a stimulant) so it can handle the flow again. The feeling of overload disappears because the router now has the energy to do its job. You can absolutely keep saying, “ADHD makes me feel overstimulated.” That is a 100% accurate description of the experience. The only reason to learn the other half of the story is to understand why a **stimulant** (the last thing you’d give an “overstimulated” person) is what provides the calm—because it’s not adding to the chaos, it’s finally giving the brain’s filter the fuel it needs to turn the volume down.
